WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.910
Look up on a clear day, and the sky is blue. But sunlight itself is white.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.100
So where does the blue come from?

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.884
The answer is written in the air itself. Tiny molecules of nitrogen and oxygen,

00:00:12.884 --> 00:00:15.300
far smaller than a wave of light.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.719
When sunlight hits them, they scatter short blue wavelengths in every direction,

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:23.225
several times more strongly than red.

00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:27.798
That scattered blue light reaches your eyes from everywhere overhead.

00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:30.250
That is why the whole sky glows blue.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.738
Violet is scattered even more, but there is less of it in sunlight,

00:00:34.738 --> 00:00:37.350
and your eyes are less sensitive to it.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:42.240
At sunset, sunlight crosses much more air. The blue is scattered away,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.475
and the reds and oranges remain.

00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:47.786
Dust and pollution scatter all colors equally,

00:00:47.786 --> 00:00:51.025
which is why a hazy sky looks pale and white.

00:00:51.025 --> 00:00:55.731
So the blue sky is not a thing. It is sunlight, rearranged by the air.

00:00:55.731 --> 00:00:57.075
Thanks for watching.
